<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rapperswil</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rapperswil is a town on the northern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-zurich">Lake Zürich</a> in the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, dominated by its medieval castle. It belonged to the Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-laufenburg">Habsburg-Laufenburg</a>, and after they supported an attempted coup against Zürich in 1350, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-brun">Rudolf Brun</a> destroyed the town. It later came under the protection of the Swiss confederates, and since 2007 it has formed part of Rapperswil-Jona.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rapperswil.jpg" alt="Rapperswil" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland zh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rapperswil_-_Seedamm_-_Holzbr%C3%BCcke_-_Etzel_Kulm_2010-10-21_16-39-22.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rapperswil lies on the northern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-zurich">Lake Zurich</a>, at the point where the Seedamm isthmus, an ice age moraine, cuts the lake in two. The upper, eastern part is called the Obersee. Part of the old town, the castle and a monastery stand on a peninsula. The town is often called the town of roses because of its rose gardens, where about 15,000 plants of 600 kinds can be seen between June and October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement in the area goes back at least 5,000 years. Remains of a first wooden bridge to Hurden, dated to 1523 BCE, and neighbouring prehistoric pile dwellings are part of the UNESCO World Heritage Site Prehistoric Pile Dwellings around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. At Kempraten the Roman vicus Centum Prata became an important trading centre in the 1st century CE.</p><p>Rapperswil Castle was built around 1200 by the Counts of Rapperswil, Rudolf II and his son Rudolf III, and the town was founded when the family moved across the lake from Altendorf. The last countess of the house, Elisabeth von Rapperswil, died in 1309. Her grandson Johann II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-laufenburg">Habsburg-Laufenburg</a> led the opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-brun">Rudolf Brun</a>, the mayor of Zurich, and in 1350 Brun destroyed the town walls and the castle, which Albrecht II, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, rebuilt in 1352/54.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iv-duke-of-austria">Rudolf IV, Duke of Austria</a>, built a wooden bridge across the lake between 1358 and 1360, about 1,450 metres long, which was used until 1878. The town bought its freedom in 1415, and in 1458 it came under the Swiss Confederation as a common lordship of Glarus and Schwyz. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a> of 1803 it joined the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>On 1 January 2007 Rapperswil and Jona merged into the municipality of Rapperswil-Jona. Since 1870 the castle has housed the Polish National Museum, founded by Polish exiles including Count Wladyslaw Broel-Plater. The castle, the medieval town hall and the Seedamm area with the Heilig Hüsli chapel are listed as Swiss heritage sites of national significance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rapperswil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Threatened by exiled councillors, who attempted a coup in 1350 with the support of the Count of Habsburg-Laufenburg at <b>Rapperswil</b>, Brun concluded a perpetual alliance with Lucerne, Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden on 1 May 1351, bringing Zürich into the Old Swiss Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
